WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.640
Back in 2012, the Harvard Joint Center

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:06.480
for Housing Studies published a report

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.040
warning that America was heading towards

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.599
a generational housing crisis. That if

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.240
we didn't dramatically increase supply,

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:17.039
reduce regulatory costs, and address the

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.760
growing gap between wages and home

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:21.680
prices, then home ownership as we know

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.160
it would become out of reach for an

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.160
entire generation. Nobody listened, and

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.199
sure enough, everything they predicted

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:32.239
came true. Supply collapsed, costs

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:34.800
exploded, wages fell further behind, and

00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:37.040
now we're living in the consequences.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.079
The housing market is now in its least

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.800
affordable territory in 40 years. Just

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:46.480
33% of 30-year-olds own a home today,

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.399
down from 47% in 1984. And the average

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.039
age of a first-time home buyer just hit

00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:52.800
a record high of 40 years old. This

00:00:51.039 --> 00:00:54.879
isn't a slowdown, and it's not a

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.120
correction. It is a full-blown collapse

00:00:54.879 --> 00:00:59.039
that was predicted decades ago and

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.719
nobody stopped it. And here's the secret

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:02.800
that they've been keeping from us. This

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:04.559
isn't a housing story. What's happening

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.040
right now is going to permanently

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:08.960
reshape who builds wealth in America

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.960
over the next decade. The people who

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.880
understand it will come out ahead. But

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.600
the people who don't will get locked

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.119
out, not just from a home, but from the

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.200
single biggest wealthb buildinging

00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:21.439
vehicle the middle class has really ever

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.520
had. I'm going to break down exactly how

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:25.759
we got here, why it is not going away

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.200
anytime soon, and what we can do right

00:01:25.759 --> 00:01:29.200
now to make sure that we're on the right

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.479
side of this. And before we do, at any

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.240
point throughout the video, if you think

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:33.680
I've earned it, do me a huge favor and

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.200
just hit that like button. It would help

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.799
me out a ton, and it would get this

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.960
video in front of more people who need

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:41.040
to see it. For years, economists warned

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.040
that the historically low mortgage rates

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.720
of the pandemic era were creating a

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.040
ticking time bomb. that when rates

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.880
eventually rose, and they always rise,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.560
that the market would seize up in a way

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.560
that we've never seen before. And sure

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.560
enough, that is exactly what's happened.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.920
During the pandemic, mortgage rates hit

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:00.079
historic lows of 2.5%. And millions of

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.000
Americans locked those rates in. It felt

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:04.320
like a gift. But what nobody told them

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.560
was that gift was about to become a wall

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.959
that would lock everybody else out. Then

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.920
inflation hit. The Fed hiked rates, and

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:13.599
now you're looking at 6 12% on a 30-year

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.480
mortgage. And here's what that means. is

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:18.720
in real dollars. So on a $410,000 home,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.560
the current median price in America,

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.840
your monthly payment went from about

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.680
$14.46 to 2223. The house didn't get

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.080
bigger. The neighborhood didn't change

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:31.599
and nothing improved. The money just got

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.599
more expensive. That's an extra $777

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:35.360
every single month just from the rate

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:37.840
change alone. All those people who

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.000
bought at 3% are now trapped. And in the

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.080
best way for them, but the worst way for

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.480
you and me. If they sell, they'd have to

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.480
buy again at 6 1/2%. They'd be

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.200
voluntarily doubling their interest

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.680
rate. So, they're staying put. Nobody's

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.760
selling. Inventory dried up. Prices

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.080
actually stayed high. And firsttime

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.000
buyers were left with almost nothing to

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.840
choose from. The economists who warned

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.920
about this called it the lock-in effect.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.760
And right now, it has the entire housing

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.680
market in a chokeold. And this is

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.400
exactly why everybody sitting on the

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.560
sidelines waiting for prices to crash

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.800
might be waiting for a really long time.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.519
The same rates keeping buyers out are

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.519
keeping sellers in. There is no flood of

00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:18.879
inventory coming. The math just really

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:20.640
doesn't allow it. According to

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:23.200
realtor.com, in the third quarter of

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.680
2025, new construction mortgage rates

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.519
averaged 5.27%.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.239
Which is roughly a full percentage point

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:32.000
lower than the 6 1.5% rate on existing

00:03:30.239 --> 00:03:33.760
homes. And in a lot of markets right

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.920
now, a brand new home is actually

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.080
cheaper per month than going and buying

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.000
a resale home. They're literally paying

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.519
you to get into a new home. Here's the

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.040
problem that predates everything else.

00:03:41.519 --> 00:03:45.040
And in a lot of ways, it's the most

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:47.120
important one. Depending on who you ask,

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.120
we're short somewhere between 3 and 6

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:51.040
million homes. The exact number doesn't

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.440
matter as much. What matters is the

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.680
underlying problem. We built way too

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.920
little for way too long. Here's how it

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.680
happened. After the 2008 financial

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.920
crisis, some of you remember it.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.319
Construction fell off a cliff and the

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.159
recovery was painfully slow. The

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:09.519
industry didn't even get back to its

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:11.599
previous low point until 2012, six years

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:13.680
to stop the bleeding. Construction firms

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:17.840
and workers didn't fully recover until

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:20.320
2022, a full 14 years after the crash.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:22.800
So, when the pandemic hit and demand

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.280
exploded, there was almost nothing to

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:30.639
buy. The median home price went from

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:32.560
$317 in 2020 to $4.42 in 2022. That is a

00:04:30.639 --> 00:04:35.360
$125,000

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.560
increase in just 2 years. Rent jumped

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:40.880
from 1,600 to over $2,000 in the same

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:43.280
period. And a shortage that had quietly

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.360
been building over a decade exploded all

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.080
at one time, and we still can't build

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.400
fast enough to fix it. Since 2020, more

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.240
than 80% of construction materials have

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.800
gone up in price. There's a shortage of

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:57.280
around 500,000 construction workers, and

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.919
government regulations add roughly 94

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:02.560
grand in fixed costs to every single

00:04:59.919 --> 00:05:04.639
home built before one nail is ever

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.800
hammered. The math just simply doesn't

00:05:04.639 --> 00:05:09.440
work for affordable homes right now. And

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.440
the result is that last year, 1.6

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.759
million young households should have

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:15.360
formed, but didn't. Young adults stayed

00:05:13.759 --> 00:05:17.199
with roommates. They stayed with their

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.039
parents. They stayed wherever they could

00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:21.440
because there was nowhere affordable to

00:05:19.039 --> 00:05:24.080
actually go. Household formation hit a

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.160
9-year low in 2024. And this is not a

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.560
short-term problem. And there really is

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.400
no quick fix coming anytime soon. Even

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.320
if rates were lower and supply was

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.479
higher, we would still have a problem

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.120
because the relationship between home

00:05:34.479 --> 00:05:39.919
prices and income has fundamentally

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:42.479
broken down. In 1960, the median home

00:05:39.919 --> 00:05:45.280
cost was $11,900

00:05:42.479 --> 00:05:48.560
and the median income was $5600. That is

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:51.120
a price to income ratio of 2:1. Housing

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:53.680
economists consider 3:1 the upper limit

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.320
of affordable. Today, the median home is

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:59.280
410 grand and the median household

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:02.080
income is 80 grand. That is 5:1

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:03.840
nationally and up 10 times in major

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.319
cities. And here's what makes that even

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:10.080
scarier. Adjusted for inflation, that

00:06:06.319 --> 00:06:12.800
1960 home should cost 131,000 today.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:16.400
Instead, it costs 410,000.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:19.520
Since 1985, incomes grew 241%.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.600
But home prices grew by 408%.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.080
And according to the US Treasury, since

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:27.520
2000, inflationadjusted house prices

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:30.479
rose 65% while inflation adjusted

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:32.319
incomes barely even moved. This isn't a

00:06:30.479 --> 00:06:34.800
recent problem. This has been happening

00:06:32.319 --> 00:06:37.280
for decades and it just hit a breaking

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:38.800
point. And the result is you now need

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:40.720
$117,000

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:42.960
a year to afford the median home in

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.199
America. According to realtor.com, we

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:48.240
are now in the least affordable housing

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:50.960
market in at least 40 years. Not the

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:53.680
least affordable since 2008, not the

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:56.240
least affordable since the 2000s, 40

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:58.560
years. The bottom line, wages and home

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.199
prices used to track together, and they

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.599
don't anymore. And no policy is going to

00:07:01.199 --> 00:07:05.360
fix a 60-year-old problem overnight.

00:07:03.599 --> 00:07:07.280
Here's one that makes people angry, and

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.880
I get it because for a long time, I had

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.560
hedge funds wanting to buy properties

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.199
from my real estate company here in

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:14.960
Arizona. In 2024, investors accounted

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:16.880
for 25.7%

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:19.039
of all residential home sales in

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.199
America. That's one in every four homes

00:07:19.039 --> 00:07:23.440
sold. Not going to a family, not going

00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:25.360
to a first-time buyer, but going to an

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:27.280
investor. Private equity firms were

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:29.520
showing up to foreclosure auctions with

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.039
all cash offers that average buyers

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.039
couldn't compete with. And these

00:07:31.039 --> 00:07:34.639
investors then converted the single

00:07:33.039 --> 00:07:36.960
family homes, a lot of them, into

00:07:34.639 --> 00:07:39.120
rentals, which reduced the available

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:41.680
supply to purchase for first-time home

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.520
buyers and put upward pressure on rents

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:45.599
all at the same time. In specific

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:47.280
markets like Miami, foreign buyers

00:07:45.599 --> 00:07:49.919
accounted for over half of new

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.160
construction sales. They spent 4.4

00:07:49.919 --> 00:07:54.800
billion in South Florida alone, up from

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:57.840
3.1 billion the prior year. And that

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:00.319
accounted for 10% of all international

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.000
home sales nationwide. domestic

00:08:00.319 --> 00:08:03.919
first-time buyers weren't just competing

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.680
with other families. They were competing

00:08:03.919 --> 00:08:07.520
with corporate balance sheets and

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:09.199
foreign capital from around the world.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:11.680
Now, there's a counterargument worth

00:08:09.199 --> 00:08:13.680
hearing. Some researchers argue that

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:15.840
institutional investors make up a

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.400
relatively small portion of overall

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:20.639
inventory and they are not the primary

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:22.960
driver of the crisis. The data backs

00:08:20.639 --> 00:08:25.440
that up. Concern about investors ranked

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:27.759
third in recent surveys behind income

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:30.000
levels and mortgage rates. But here's my

00:08:27.759 --> 00:08:32.080
take. Even if institutions aren't the

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.000
number one cause, they still added fuel

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:35.599
to an already raging fire. And

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:38.479
politically, it's starting to catch up

00:08:35.599 --> 00:08:40.800
with them. In January 2026, an executive

00:08:38.479 --> 00:08:42.640
order was signed calling for a ban on

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:45.120
corporate purchases of single family

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:47.279
homes. And according to Redfin, just 29%

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.120
of home buyers paid cash in December,

00:08:47.279 --> 00:08:51.440
the lowest share for that month since

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:53.440
2020. The tide is turning on this one,

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:55.200
but the damage to a generation of

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:57.760
wouldbe buyers could have already been

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:00.240
done. The last piece is simple economics

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:02.640
and that is demand. Millennials are the

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:05.120
largest generation in American history,

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:07.920
72 million people, and they've all hit

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:10.080
peak home buying age at the same time.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.080
This wave of demand was always going to

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:14.240
test the market. The problem is it

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:16.640
arrived right when supply was at its

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:19.040
lowest point in decades. And the ripple

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:21.519
effects go way beyond housing. Americans

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.600
are now delaying every major life

00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:26.320
milestone. The average firsttime mother

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:28.640
is 30 years old. Fewer 30-year-olds live

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:30.640
on their own, get married, or own homes

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:32.560
than any previous generation. The

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.640
housing crisis isn't just keeping people

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:36.399
out of homes. It's keeping people from

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:38.480
starting their lives. Think about that

00:09:36.399 --> 00:09:40.640
conflict. The biggest generation in

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:43.040
history trying to buy homes and the

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:45.120
smallest inventory in a generation

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.120
available to buy. That's not really

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:49.440
ideal for affordability. According to

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:51.760
John Burns research, despite a declining

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:54.000
youth population, all major home buyer

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:56.959
groups are projected to grow over the

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.959
next decade. More buyers are coming. The

00:09:56.959 --> 00:10:01.600
supply is not keeping up. And until it

00:09:58.959 --> 00:10:03.200
does, prices have nowhere to go but up.

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:05.279
So, let me bring all this together for

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:06.640
you. And if you got any value so far, do

00:10:05.279 --> 00:10:08.480
me a favor and please hit that like

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:10.720
button. Four things broke the housing

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:13.200
market at the same time. Rates locked

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:15.839
existing homeowners in place. We stopped

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:17.680
building. Wages fell way behind.

00:10:15.839 --> 00:10:20.320
investors bought up a lot of inventory

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:22.560
and demand kept growing. And that right

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:24.160
there is the crisis. But here's what I

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.760
want you to walk away with. You don't

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:27.680
have to wait for the government to fix

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:29.839
it and make some type of move. My

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:32.720
recommendation is to stop waiting for a

00:10:29.839 --> 00:10:34.640
crash. On poly market, 79% of btors

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:36.720
think that home values will continue to

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:38.640
climb. Nobody's betting on prices going

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.640
down. Waiting for a crash that may never

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:42.640
come is one of the most expensive

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:44.480
mistakes that you can make. So, get your

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:46.399
financial house in order. Know your

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:48.160
credit score. build your down payment

00:10:46.399 --> 00:10:50.480
and the window will open at the right

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:52.560
time for you. And when it does, be ready

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:54.079
to move so you're not scrambling. And

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:56.399
pay attention to where you're buying,

00:10:54.079 --> 00:10:58.160
not just when. Location matters. There

00:10:56.399 --> 00:10:59.920
are a ton of cities across the country

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.000
where you can get a good deal. You can

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:04.160
go and negotiating. You can get the

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:05.839
seller to give you concessions and all

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:08.480
kinds of different stuff. If you really

